cff-version: 1.2.0
title: 'LoRaCAP: a field-calibrated LoRaWAN uplink capacity simulator'
message: >-
  If LoRaCAP contributes to published work, please cite it. If you use it
  for a specific deployment study, cite the paper reporting that study as
  well.
type: software
# NOTE for double-blind review: this file identifies the authors. Remove or
# redact it from any reviewer-facing copy, and prefer an anonymised mirror
# (for example anonymous.4open.science) or a restricted-access deposit with a
# reviewer token over a public repository.
authors:
  - given-names: Wilder Eduardo
    family-names: Castellanos Hernández
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0311-492X'
    affiliation: 'School of Engineering, Pontificia Universidad Javeriana, Bogotá, Colombia'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/wilcashe/loracap'
abstract: >-
  LoRaCAP estimates how many nodes a single-gateway LoRaWAN network supports
  on a given site before the Data Extraction Rate falls below a chosen
  threshold. A site is described in one study file: the boundary over which
  nodes are placed, its propagation model, fitted from field measurements or assumed from terrain,
  and its radio configuration. The engine is independent of any deployment.
  Its collision model re-implements LoRaSim (Bor et al., MSWiM 2016) and
  extends it with multi-channel simulation, a chip-wide limit on simultaneous
  demodulations, regional data-rate and power tables, log-normal shadowing,
  steady-state ADR and an energy model. Two receiver rules are available,
  LoRaSim's and a lock rule based on measured SX1272 and SX1301 behaviour,
  together with optional rejection between spreading factors taken from
  published measurements. The engine is validated twice. It degenerates onto
  the analytical pure-ALOHA bound when the mechanisms the theory lacks are
  switched off, and it agrees with LoRaSim within 0.009 DER on Bor et al.'s
  Experiment Set 1. Capacity is reported with a bootstrap confidence
  interval. Every quoted result is regenerated from a manifest that fixes its
  configuration and seed, and every output file records the digest of the
  code that produced it. Written in Python 3 with the standard library only,
  plus matplotlib for figures. Includes tools to convert a KML boundary and to
  fit a log-distance model to field measurements, and a web interface for
  configuring a study.
keywords:
  - LoRaWAN
  - LPWAN
  - network scalability
  - discrete-event simulation
  - path loss
  - Internet of Things
license: MIT
license-url: 'https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT'
